    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the general business law, in relation to certain rental vehicle protections",
    "basePrintNo": "S4711",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S4711-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §396-z, Gen Bus L",
    "lawSection": "General Business Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S4711\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the general business law, in\nrelation to certain rental vehicle protections\n\n PURPOSE OF THE BILL :\n\nTo update the rental vehicle consumer protections contained in § 396-z\nof the General Business Law to better reflect the manner in which\nvehicle rental transactions are executed while still maintaining the\nessential consumer rights and notification provisions of the existing\nlaw.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\n\n(1) Makes changes throughout the law to clarify the use of the term\n\"renter\" and \"authorized driver\" . A renter is the person or entity\nwho entered into the contract with a rental vehicle company. An\nauthorized driver is the renter, the renter 's spouse, and any person\nnoted on the contract as an additional driver . The respective rights\nand obligations of each are not always the same, yet existing law does\nnot consistently reflect those differences.\n\n(2) Amends § 396-z(1) to delete the confusing reference to \"private\npassenger vehicle\" currently relied upon in § 396-z(1)(e) to\ndifferentiate between commercial uses excluded from the provision and\nprivate passenger uses covered by the provision; and clearly specifies\nin a new paragraph (k) that commercial vehicles \"used or maintained\nprimarily for the transportation of property\" are excluded from the\nstatute.\n\n(3) Amends § 396-z(2)(a) to reorganize the tiers for optional vehicle\nprotection (OVP) to reflect current marketplace conditions. For the\npast ten years, the daily price of optional vehicle protection has\nbeen capped at $9.00 if the vehicle has an MSRP of not more than\n$30,000 and $12.00/day if the vehicle has an MSRP of more than\n$30,000.  Under the new tier schedule, OVP will be capped at: $9 if\nthe MSRP is not greater than $20,000; $12 if the MSRP is greater than\n$20,000 but less than $35,000; $15 if the MSRP is greater than $35,000\nbut less than $50,000; and may be charged at market value for vehicles\nwith an MSRP of more than $50,000. The bill would also provide for\nannual adjustments to the capped charges based on the Consumer Price\nIndex.  The changes are necessitated by a combination of standard\ninflation over the past 10 years, changes in the manner by which\nrental cars are purchased from manufacturers, and the increased costs\nassociated with repairing vehicles today.\n\nUnder the pre-2004 statutory scheme, rental companies could not afford\nto rent cars worth more than $30,000, and most cars available in New\nYork were absolute base models. Today, the new rental environment\nmakes vehicles available that consumers want and expect, p4,greatly\nexceed $30,000 in value. Logically, a $12 cap is not sufficient to\ncover OVP loses related to these more expensive vehicles.\nNevertheless, under these amendments, approximately 80% of cars rented\nin New York would still fit under the $9 or $12 cap.\n\nThe overwhelming majority of states simply allow the marketplace to\nset the price of OVP. In these states, the market value of damage\nwaiver is between $18 and 24 per day, even for base-model car.  This\nbill would maintain the intent of original legislation by placing tier\ncaps on all but a handful of super-luxury vehicles and would provide\nfor an annual adjustments based on the CPI. Based on an analysis of\nthe 15 years since GBL 396-z was amended, these annual adjustments\nwould generally be just a few pennies a year.\n\n\n(4) Amends § 396-z(2)(d) to provide a means for a customer to\nterminate optional vehicle protection after 24 hours. Currently, the\nlaw only provides a mechanism for terminating the OVP within the first\n24 hours of purchase.\n\n(5) Amends § 396-z(3) to specify the right and obligations of a renter\nand rental vehicle company when a vehicle is \"recovered\" (instead of\n\"returned\") or returned during non-business hours. The current law\nonly anticipates the situation where a vehicle is returned during\nnormal business hours.\n\n(6) Amends § 396-z(4)(e) to update the telephone disclosure\nrequirement regarding optional vehicle protection. Current\nrequirements have led to more confusion than clarification. This\nchange recognizes that there are numerous optional products available\nfor purchase, but provides that any discussion of OVP contain the\ninformation required by the statute.\n\n(7) Amends § 396-z (4)(f) to clearly specify exactly what the rental\nvehicle company must disclose on signs or pamphlets regarding\nconsumers' rights and obligations. Current law does not specifically\nindicate what information rental vehicle companies are required to\ndisclose in the rental agreement and on clearly visible signs or\npamphlets at the rental location making enforcement of disclosure\ncompliance difficult. This would provide consumers with uniformity and\na sense of clarity and consistency regarding their rights regardless\nof which rental vehicle company they choose.\n\n(8) Amends § 396-z((5)to unravel currently-confusing language relating\nto the rights of a renter and/or the renter's insurance company to\ninspect a damaged vehicle, and the time periods controlling this\nprocess, while maintaining all the existing time periods or notice\nrequirements of the existing provision.\n\n(9) Amends § 396-z(6) to clarify that damages related to loss of use\nand administrative fees may not be assessed against a renter or\nauthorized driver, but may be assessed against a negligent third\nparty. Also adds clarity to, and removes redundancies from how a\ndamage claim is presented to the customer's insurance carrier.\n\n(10) Adds a new subdivision 16 to § 396-z to provide for the\ndisclosure requirements to be delivered electronically when a customer\nutilizes a technology for the rental that bypasses the counter. More\nand more customers are using Internet programs or Apps to secure their\nrental vehicle, and as such, do not have to appear at the rental\ncounter. This provision assures that the customer will still encounter\nthe disclosures required under the General Business Law.\n\n(11) Makes other technical and clarifying provisions and removes\nredundancies throughout the statute.\n\n(12) Removes the 2018 sunset provision (which would have represented\nthe fourth five-year extension.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nChapter 656 of the Laws of 2004 represents the very core or the\nrelationship between rental car companies and New York consumers. For\nalmost 15 years, this statute has allowed the rental car industry to\ngrow in New York, while providing its customers with the most\ncomprehensive set of consumer protections anywhere in the country and\nbroad range of vehicle options. The results have proven beneficial for\nthe consumer and the industry; the industry has expanded into\npreviously underserved areas, and robust competition between the\nvarious rental companies has provided consumers with a variety of\naffordable options.\nMoreover, sale of the damage waiver as an optional product is\npermitted, but subject to significant and comprehensive disclosure and\neducation regarding the consumer's options. As a result, the original\nfive-year trial was deemed a success and extended in 2008, and then\nagain in 2013. The law has become so concretely embedded into the\nfabric of New York law that reversion to the pre-2004 law would\nreverse years of a mutually-beneficial set of norms that have served\nas a national model. Since Chapter 656 has turned out to be\nexceptionally beneficial to the consumer and industry alike, it is\ntime to make it permanent and provide the industry with an important\nmeasure of clarity and stability regarding its continued growth in New\nYork.\n\nThe changes embodied by this legislation are timely and necessary.\nAfter almost 15 years of working within the framework of Chapter 656,\nsome of the requirements of the law have proven to be confusing for\nconsumers and difficult to execute for the rental companies.\nMoreover, the statute has not kept up with technology advances in the\ndelivery of the rental product. This measure clarifies some of its\nmore ambiguous and/or unworkable provisions of the law to the benefit\nof the consumers and rental companies alike, while, at the same time,\nupdating some of its provisions to reflect delivery mechanisms\nubiquitous to today's marketplace - but never envisioned in 2004.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nSimilar to A. 8159 / S. 5743 of 2016 (Morelle/Klein) Remained in\nSenate and Assembly Consumer Protection Committee\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThe act will take effect on the ninetieth day after which is shall\nhave become law.\n",
